%description:

Tcp.acceptBeyondBufferAtEmptyQueue:accept -- a segment larger than the buffer gets in at an empty queue.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "accept a whole in-order segment that reaches beyond the receive buffer
when the receive queue is empty, as Linux does (tcp_data_queue()); the window is then zero
until the application reads. false: drop such a segment".

The classes: drop (false, the default) and accept (true, the value of LinuxTcp). The code:
TcpConnection::hasEnoughSpaceForSegmentInReceiveQueue() takes an in-order segment at an
empty receive queue whole with true; with false, the segment must end within the buffer. This test separates "accept" from "drop".

Host under test: host2, with a receive buffer of 1000 bytes (precondition), whose application
reads at once; the tap writes 65535 into the window field of each segment of host2, so that
host1 sends segments of 1448 bytes, which are larger than the buffer. Observation: level 1, host2 acknowledges data.

%# parameter: Tcp.acceptBeyondBufferAtEmptyQueue
%# class: accept
%# value: true
%# key: *.host2.tcp.acceptBeyondBufferAtEmptyQueue
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpAcceptBeyondBufferAtEmptyQueueAccept")
        .intercept(tap("tap").from("host2").filterPacket("tcp.ackBit == true").set("tcp.window", "65535")
                     .describe("each segment of host2: the window field 65535, so that host1 sends beyond the offer of host2"))
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
             .capture("isn", "tcp.sequenceNo")
             .describe("the SYN of host1")
             .within(0.5))
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.ackNo > {isn} + 1")
             .describe("the first ACK of data")
             .within(1.0));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpAcceptBeyondBufferAtEmptyQueueAccept compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host2.tcp.acceptBeyondBufferAtEmptyQueue = true    # the parameter under test
*.host2.tcp.receiveBufferSize = 1000B
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 4344B
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpAcceptBeyondBufferAtEmptyQueueAccept: PASS
